WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:07.170
SIERRA: Hip Hop.

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:08.560
We all know it.

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:12.330
And we can’t turn on the radio or go to
a party without hearing it.

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:18.539
It’s an American artform - born in the Bronx,
and audible from coast to coast.

00:00:18.539 --> 00:00:21.850
But in recent decades, hip hop has gone global.

00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:30.050
And at this point, almost every country on
earth has developed its own style of the genre.

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:41.020
[Global Hip-hop Montage]

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:53.840
SIERRA: Hip-Hop is a global culture.

00:00:53.840 --> 00:01:01.050
It's also a multi-billion dollar business
fueled by artists all over the world.

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:08.900
Still, American artists dominate the genre,
and this presents a unique opportunity for

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:10.369
cultural diplomacy.

00:01:10.369 --> 00:01:16.160
Yeah, it may be surprising, but for years,
the US State Department has been capitalizing

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:21.630
on the power of hip-hop, sending musical ambassadors
abroad to forge connections.

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:25.130
I’m Gabrielle Sierra and this is Why It
Matters.

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:35.540
Today, hip-hop diplomacy, and the enduring
legacy of America’s musical soft power.

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:46.479
BLACKMAN: I think the concept behind soft
power is that instead of being aggressive

00:01:46.479 --> 00:01:51.990
or right in someone's face, is that you are
more subtle with the idea of relationship

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:52.990
building.

00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:55.130
SIERRA: This is Toni Blackman.

00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:59.469
She’s a rapper, an educator, and cultural
ambassador.

00:01:59.469 --> 00:02:03.000
She was the first Hip Hop Diplomat for the
U.S. State Department.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:07.950
Today she continues to do official musical
diplomacy, with an organization called Next

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:11.900
Level, and another that she founded: Rhyme
like a Girl.

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:16.620
BLACKMAN: And so, eventually I believe the
way it connects to foreign policy is that

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:23.370
policy decisions are made not only based upon
the policy itself, but rooted in relationships,

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:24.370
right?

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:30.769
It's very easy for musicians and artists to
be able to create a dialogue, and that dialogue

00:02:30.769 --> 00:02:38.720
then spreads out, in terms of peoples' attitudes,
and impressions, and their view, their perspective

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:42.090
on America.

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:50.010
SIERRA: For more than a hundred years, American
music and pop culture have been remarkably

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:51.150
popular abroad.

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:58.980
I mean think about it, Americans created blues,
jazz, rock and roll, motown, R&amp;B, and now

00:02:58.980 --> 00:02:59.980
hip-hop.

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:05.900
And with each of these waves, musicians abroad
were inspired to create their own versions.

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:11.340
All along, the State Department has attempted
to capitalize on this popularity, and consolidate

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:16.810
this musical soft power via diplomatic programs
that sponsored concerts and cultural exchanges

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:17.940
around the world.

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:24.700
BLACKMAN: It’s about advancing the interest
of the country, at the end of the day.

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:27.950
But, it's also about improving the relationships.

00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:31.420
There's a humanitarian aspect to the work.

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:36.690
And, it's one of the reasons why I admire
foreign service workers so much.

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:38.790
The end goal really is about influence.

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:44.090
It is about power, but it's also about culture,
and it's about connection and community.

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:46.120
And promoting peace.

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:51.319
SIERRA: You have the coolest job that maybe
I've ever heard in the whole world, so I can't

00:03:51.319 --> 00:03:52.780
wait to dive into it.

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:56.069
But before we do, how would you define hip-hop?

00:03:56.069 --> 00:04:01.480
BLACKMAN: I would define hip-hop as a music
and art culture.

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:06.150
A lot of time, when people use the term hip-hop
they think about rap.

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:09.879
But, rap is only one small part of what hip-hop
music and culture is.

00:04:09.879 --> 00:04:16.250
There is the rap, there is the DJing, there
is dance, there is the visual art.

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:19.450
Not only graffiti writing, but in terms of
mural making.

00:04:19.450 --> 00:04:21.160
And, fashion design.

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:24.840
Fashion is a big part of what hip-hop culture
has given birth to.

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:29.970
There is vocal percussion, better known as
beatboxing and making music with your mouth.

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:35.000
And so, when people teach hip-hop 101 they
say, "There are four core elements of hip-hop.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:37.640
Rap, dance, DJing, and graffiti."

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:43.630
But, now it's expanded over the years and
there's a whole movement globally of hip-hop

00:04:43.630 --> 00:04:49.700
heads, of artists who are committed to doing
activism around climate change and spiritual

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:51.639
and mental health.

00:04:51.639 --> 00:05:01.100
KATZ: Hip hop is a cultural form that originated
in the United States but is strongly connected

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:09.320
with African-American and African diasporic
cultures around the world and grew up in the

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:18.090
1970s and is about to celebrate its 50th birthday
if you take the usual birth date of August

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:22.300
11th, 1973, as its official birthday.

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:27.340
SIERRA: This is Mark Katz, he is an author
and a professor of music at the University

00:05:27.340 --> 00:05:28.639
of North Carolina.

00:05:28.639 --> 00:05:33.819
He’s the founder of Next Level, and has
worked closely with Toni on musical diplomacy

00:05:33.819 --> 00:05:34.819
for years.

00:05:34.819 --> 00:05:36.800
SIERRA: What's that day?

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:43.759
KATZ: So that was the day that is considered
the first hip hop party that was thrown by

00:05:43.759 --> 00:05:49.280
DJ Kool Herc in an apartment building in the
south Bronx.

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:55.850
And although it wasn't called hip hop at the
time, we look back at that moment as an event

00:05:55.850 --> 00:05:59.470
that started what hip hop became.

00:05:59.470 --> 00:06:03.460
SIERRA: Can you tell me a little bit about
what you do?

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:10.850
KATZ: Since 2013, I have been associated with
what I consider to be a very important cultural

00:06:10.850 --> 00:06:17.620
diplomacy program run out of the State Department's
Bureau of Educational and Cultural Affairs.

00:06:17.620 --> 00:06:23.870
And the program is called Next Level, and
it sends US hip hop artists around the world

00:06:23.870 --> 00:06:29.979
to make people-to-people connections with
citizens of other countries, to promote a

00:06:29.979 --> 00:06:33.880
more positive image of the US, to create mutual
understanding.

00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:40.199
And I have had the pleasure and privilege
of traveling to dozens of countries around

00:06:40.199 --> 00:06:48.229
the world with this program and being able
to see up close how music brings people together

00:06:48.229 --> 00:06:51.580
who might otherwise have no real connections.

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:56.340
SIERRA: Hip-hop’s global popularity didn’t
just spring up overnight.

00:06:56.340 --> 00:06:57.729
It was a long road.

00:06:57.729 --> 00:07:03.319
So here’s a quick history for those who
need it: In 1979, The Sugar Hill Gang released

00:07:03.319 --> 00:07:13.720
Rappers Delight, the first hip hop single
to chart in the Top 40.

00:07:13.720 --> 00:07:20.009
In the 1980s, hip hop artists like LL Cool
J, Salt N Peppa, Rakim and Public Enemy took

00:07:20.009 --> 00:07:22.080
the genre to new heights.

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:28.770
In 1986, Run DMC released the first platinum
hip hop album, gaining crossover appeal through

00:07:28.770 --> 00:07:30.870
a collaboration with Aerosmith.

00:07:30.870 --> 00:07:37.270
By the 1990s, hip hop gained notoriety for
perceived misogyny and violence in rap lyrics.

00:07:37.270 --> 00:07:41.949
At the same time, a series of groundbreaking
rappers rose to global prominence.

00:07:41.949 --> 00:07:49.210
Tupac Shakur and the Notorious BIG both released
albums that sold more than 10 million copies

00:07:49.210 --> 00:07:53.530
worldwide, that’s a platinum record ten
times over.

00:07:53.530 --> 00:07:58.490
At the end of the decade, Lauryn Hill became
the first hip hop artist to receive a Grammy

00:07:58.490 --> 00:08:00.449
for Album of the year.

00:08:00.449 --> 00:08:05.330
By the early 2000s, Eminem and Outkast had
also released diamond records, and it was

00:08:05.330 --> 00:08:11.060
becoming commonplace to hear these artists
celebrated on radio stations around the world.

00:08:11.060 --> 00:08:16.849
As love for the genre grew, hip hop scenes
in places like France, the UK, and many parts

00:08:16.849 --> 00:08:21.530
of Africa were maturing in their own right,
setting the groundwork for global hip hop

00:08:21.530 --> 00:08:23.599
as we know it today.

00:08:23.599 --> 00:08:30.720
Last year hip hop and R&amp;B recordings were
streamed more than 290 billion times worldwide.

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:37.760
BLACKMAN: I've been to one city in the world
on planet Earth where they didn't know who

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:38.760
Tupac was.

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:41.200
They didn't know the references.

00:08:41.200 --> 00:08:45.010
That was on the island of Aceh in Indonesia.

00:08:45.010 --> 00:08:49.480
It was fascinating to me because that was
the only time in my life where I had that

00:08:49.480 --> 00:08:50.480
occurrence.

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:51.480
But, it's big.

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:53.330
And it's expressed in different ways, in different
cultures.

00:08:53.330 --> 00:08:56.850
But, the culture is very much alive around
the world.

00:08:56.850 --> 00:09:03.149
SIERRA: Do you think that there are themes
in hip hop that make it particularly relatable

00:09:03.149 --> 00:09:04.970
to so many different people worldwide?

00:09:04.970 --> 00:09:09.970
KATZ: There are a lot of different themes
that connect with people around the world

00:09:09.970 --> 00:09:15.390
who may have never been to the US or speak
English or have any particular connection

00:09:15.390 --> 00:09:17.320
to American culture.

00:09:17.320 --> 00:09:24.840
And that’s because hip hop on the global
stage connects with very deeply held human

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:35.390
drives, the drive to express oneself, the
drive to celebrate one's joys and wins and

00:09:35.390 --> 00:09:44.350
victories, the drive to protest injustice,
and simply the drive to show what it means

00:09:44.350 --> 00:09:47.750
to be a human living in the world.

00:09:47.750 --> 00:09:49.820
And hip hop does that really well.

00:09:49.820 --> 00:09:57.450
It does that because anyone can access hip
hop, anyone who has a voice and a body can

00:09:57.450 --> 00:10:05.230
create hip hop, and because it is connected
to a community of people in the US who had

00:10:05.230 --> 00:10:13.060
to fight for recognition to get their voice
heard and creates a sense of solidarity among

00:10:13.060 --> 00:10:19.750
people around the world who find themselves
in similarly oppressed situations.

00:10:19.750 --> 00:10:24.750
SIERRA: Across borders, people have coalesced
around hip hop for decades.

00:10:24.750 --> 00:10:31.029
One example was in 1991, Serbians living under
the Milošević regime, connected to the lyrics

00:10:31.029 --> 00:10:35.860
of Public Enemy’s Fight the Power, it was
broadcast on repeat when traditional news

00:10:35.860 --> 00:10:37.010
was banned.

00:10:37.010 --> 00:10:43.950
KATZ: But it's particularly powerful for people
who don't have a voice that is recognized

00:10:43.950 --> 00:10:50.029
outside of their immediate community or people
whose voices have been silenced or whose communities

00:10:50.029 --> 00:10:52.930
have been marginalized or oppressed.

00:10:52.930 --> 00:10:57.889
So that's the real connection that people
find that they're able to express their way

00:10:57.889 --> 00:10:58.889
of life.

00:10:58.889 --> 00:11:03.490
And not simply as protest, hip hop is often
talked about as protest music.

00:11:03.490 --> 00:11:06.180
And that is true, but that's not all it is.

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:07.589
It's also party music.

00:11:07.589 --> 00:11:15.890
It's also music that recognizes one's humanity
and celebrates just being alive.

00:11:15.890 --> 00:11:23.210
SIERRA: The end result of this is that hundreds
of millions of people worldwide love hip hop.

00:11:23.210 --> 00:11:28.590
And while we don’t usually talk about concepts
like love on this show, that kind of passion

00:11:28.590 --> 00:11:31.030
is an important basis for soft power.

00:11:31.030 --> 00:11:36.280
It’s an attachment that goes deep into many
people’s identities, and it opens up a door

00:11:36.280 --> 00:11:41.810
for dialogue, and for nuanced perceptions
about what the United States means.

00:11:41.810 --> 00:11:46.720
What’s interesting about this is that it
has happened many times before.

00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:51.840
American music has a knack for capturing the
world’s heart - and as a result, the United

00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:57.040
States government has long been in the business
of using music to promote its interests around

00:11:57.040 --> 00:12:00.890
the globe.

00:12:00.890 --> 00:12:03.661
Starting in 1956, the US State Dept.

00:12:03.661 --> 00:12:10.010
sent world famous musicians like Dizzy Gilepse,
Louis Armstrong, and Duke Elington abroad

00:12:10.010 --> 00:12:11.010
as jazz ambassadors.

00:12:11.010 --> 00:12:12.720
Congressman Adam Clayton Powell Jr: We’re
going to shift the emphasis to jazz and send

00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:24.780
these artists over where they can reach the
masses of the people in Asia and Africa and

00:12:24.780 --> 00:12:28.470
one of the people who we’re planning to
use is my friend Dizzy Gillespie.

00:12:28.470 --> 00:12:30.660
Reporter: What do you think about that, Dizzy?

00:12:30.660 --> 00:12:36.699
Dizzy Gillespie: The weapon that we will use
is the cool one.

00:12:36.699 --> 00:12:43.220
SIERRA: Can you tell me a bit of the history
of US music abroad and how it's intersected

00:12:43.220 --> 00:12:45.540
with geopolitics and diplomacy?

00:12:45.540 --> 00:12:53.430
KATZ: Cultural diplomacy and in particular
musical diplomacy has always been most strongly

00:12:53.430 --> 00:13:01.110
backed and most frequently deployed in times
of threats to the US.

00:13:01.110 --> 00:13:07.589
That at the height of those threats was also
the height of the State Department's deployment

00:13:07.589 --> 00:13:14.350
of music and culture more generally abroad
as a way of using soft power.

00:13:14.350 --> 00:13:20.980
So in the '30s, the State Department sent
the composer Aaron Copland to South America

00:13:20.980 --> 00:13:26.810
to facilitate the good neighbor policy, to
make friends with South American countries

00:13:26.810 --> 00:13:30.500
so that they wouldn't be taken over by fascists.

00:13:30.500 --> 00:13:35.519
And of course, that's a simplification, but
basically that's what it was.

00:13:35.519 --> 00:13:41.339
And then during the '50s after World War II
during the cold war, it was about pushing

00:13:41.339 --> 00:13:42.880
back against communism.

00:13:42.880 --> 00:13:49.570
And during that time, particularly the cold
war period, was when American popular music

00:13:49.570 --> 00:13:53.940
came to be the primary medium of musical diplomacy.

00:13:53.940 --> 00:14:02.000
Willis Conover: Hello Louis Armstrong, the
name which means America to more people than

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:06.030
anyone else I can think of, over a long period
of time.

00:14:06.030 --> 00:14:10.940
What can you say about Louis, expect Louis
take it.

00:14:10.940 --> 00:14:14.920
Louis Armstrong: Well…whatcha say gizzard[laughter]
ah my man!

00:14:14.920 --> 00:14:18.459
KATZ: Before that it was actually more classical
music.

00:14:18.459 --> 00:14:26.529
But starting in the '50s, a lot of jazz was
sent around the world as a way of creating

00:14:26.529 --> 00:14:29.860
goodwill and doing a couple of things.

00:14:29.860 --> 00:14:36.050
One was demonstrating that the US actually
had culture and pushing back against the Soviet

00:14:36.050 --> 00:14:42.310
Union, which has a long history of operas,
ballet, symphonies and so on, and showing

00:14:42.310 --> 00:14:50.079
a American born art form which was jazz, but
also in using jazz artists, many of whom are

00:14:50.079 --> 00:14:57.889
African-American, as a way of demonstrating
the freedom that the US likes to say it has

00:14:57.889 --> 00:15:00.839
and gives all of its citizens.

00:15:00.839 --> 00:15:03.990
SIERRA: And the crowds loved it.

00:15:03.990 --> 00:15:05.453
[1965, Hungarian crowd cheers for Louis Armstrong]

00:15:05.453 --> 00:15:09.871
SIERRA: That crowd, by the way, was cheering
Louis Armstrong in Budapest, Hungary, a country

00:15:09.871 --> 00:15:16.149
under Soviet sway where the opportunity to
see a western star was very rare indeed.

00:15:16.149 --> 00:15:22.120
All around the world, jazz had come to symbolize
ideas of American freedom and its popularity

00:15:22.120 --> 00:15:24.550
made Soviet leaders nervous.

00:15:24.550 --> 00:15:29.500
During this time, radio hosts like Willis
Conover of Voice of America would transmit

00:15:29.500 --> 00:15:33.779
jazz across the iron curtain via shortwave
technology.

00:15:33.779 --> 00:15:39.329
Picture young, jazz-loving soviets tinkering
with their radio dials in private, hoping

00:15:39.329 --> 00:15:44.149
to catch a few minutes of forbidden music.

00:15:44.149 --> 00:15:50.559
SIERRA: It seems important to me that this
music has been celebrated by the world, even

00:15:50.559 --> 00:15:56.060
used as a metaphor, as you say, for American
freedom, while these Black Americans were,

00:15:56.060 --> 00:15:59.310
are, struggling for justice and recognition
in their home country.

00:15:59.310 --> 00:16:02.040
So what thoughts do you have about that dynamic?

00:16:02.040 --> 00:16:08.190
KATZ: Well, that has always been a really
complex dynamic, one that African-Americans

00:16:08.190 --> 00:16:14.339
have been dealing with in many aspects of
life, representing their country but not feeling

00:16:14.339 --> 00:16:16.130
represented by them.

00:16:16.130 --> 00:16:23.390
So soldiers going off to war, civil servants,
teachers, and many other walks of life you

00:16:23.390 --> 00:16:24.759
can say the same thing.

00:16:24.759 --> 00:16:30.760
But in terms of music, this was very public
because these tours were covered by the media

00:16:30.760 --> 00:16:32.509
around the world.

00:16:32.509 --> 00:16:40.819
And so you have these juxtapositions of Emmett
Till being lynched and famous black jazz artists

00:16:40.819 --> 00:16:43.149
performing around the world.

00:16:43.149 --> 00:16:51.120
You had all these really stark juxtapositions
of what was touted as freedom when these musicians

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:59.020
were abroad, but then really horrific examples
of racism and oppression at home.

00:16:59.020 --> 00:17:02.260
So this was something that the artist had
to navigate.

00:17:02.260 --> 00:17:08.490
They had to decide whether they felt it was
appropriate for them personally to represent

00:17:08.490 --> 00:17:15.730
their country given their lived experience,
whether it worked for them to accept regardless

00:17:15.730 --> 00:17:18.470
of the oppression that they might have faced.

00:17:18.470 --> 00:17:25.260
SIERRA: In fact, in 1962 Louis Armstrong and
Dave Brubeck released a song that expressed

00:17:25.260 --> 00:17:28.679
their conflicted feelings about representing
the U.S. abroad.

00:17:28.679 --> 00:17:32.390
Louis Armstrong: Who's the real ambassador?

00:17:32.390 --> 00:17:38.770
Certain facts we can ignore; In my humble
way, I'm the USA!

00:17:38.770 --> 00:17:49.900
Though I represent the government, the government
don't represent some policies I'm for!

00:17:49.900 --> 00:17:57.559
KATZ: It's a complex calculus that I see still
happening today with hip hop artists who have

00:17:57.559 --> 00:18:04.200
to ask themselves, do I want to be associated
with the government given how I feel the government

00:18:04.200 --> 00:18:06.670
has treated my community?

00:18:06.670 --> 00:18:08.600
And if so, why would I do that?

00:18:08.600 --> 00:18:13.720
BLACKMAN: While they were representing America,
they also challenged America, in that, "I'm

00:18:13.720 --> 00:18:17.240
good enough to represent you, but I'm not
good enough to walk through the front door

00:18:17.240 --> 00:18:18.820
as a Black person."

00:18:18.820 --> 00:18:20.820
Like, those questions were raised.

00:18:20.820 --> 00:18:25.600
And then, they were able to see the world
themselves and to see how the world embraced

00:18:25.600 --> 00:18:29.559
jazz music and jazz culture, another American
export.

00:18:29.559 --> 00:18:34.549
But, that jazz diplomacy opened the door for
hip-hop diplomacy because there was a point

00:18:34.549 --> 00:18:39.250
where the younger generations weren't tuning
into jazz, they weren't tuning into Americana

00:18:39.250 --> 00:18:42.870
and bluegrass, but they were listening to
hip-hop.

00:18:42.870 --> 00:18:48.320
SIERRA: I'd love to ask you a little bit more
about what you mentioned with the protest

00:18:48.320 --> 00:18:49.900
aspect of hip hop.

00:18:49.900 --> 00:18:53.130
Have you observed that playing out in interesting
ways abroad?

00:18:53.130 --> 00:18:57.720
You know, is there an interesting connection
between protest rap abroad and the outrage

00:18:57.720 --> 00:19:01.200
and oppression that's part of the DNA of American
hip hop?

00:19:01.200 --> 00:19:07.620
KATZ: There is a strong connection between
hip hop and protest, and that's in part because

00:19:07.620 --> 00:19:11.440
of the mythos that surrounds the birth of
hip hop.

00:19:11.440 --> 00:19:17.980
Hip hop emerged in the US out of young people,
from communities of color, largely people

00:19:17.980 --> 00:19:21.570
who had traditionally been oppressed within
their own country.

00:19:21.570 --> 00:19:27.039
And so there is a knowledge not only that
hip hop is American but that it was born in

00:19:27.039 --> 00:19:34.640
a way as a form of protest and of celebrating
life in difficult circumstances.

00:19:34.640 --> 00:19:41.630
SIERRA: Songs by NWA and Public Enemy have
consistently been used in global protest movements

00:19:41.630 --> 00:19:43.460
since they emerged in the 1980s.

00:19:43.460 --> 00:19:50.299
Public Enemy: Fight the power, fight the power,
fight the power.

00:19:50.299 --> 00:19:55.809
SIERRA: And During the Arab Spring, an artist
named Hamada Ben Amor used rap to criticize

00:19:55.809 --> 00:19:57.250
the Tunisian government.

00:19:57.250 --> 00:20:01.059
The song he wrote went viral, and became an
anthem for the movement.

00:20:01.059 --> 00:20:06.179
During this era, artists like Omar Offendum
rapped in both Arabic and English to bring

00:20:06.179 --> 00:20:07.919
the world’s attention to the revolution.

00:20:07.919 --> 00:20:11.420
Omar Offendum: Freedom isn't given by oppressors/It's
demanded by oppressed/Freedom lovers, freedom

00:20:11.420 --> 00:20:16.420
fighters/Free to gather and protest/For their
God-given rights/For the freedom of the press/We

00:20:16.420 --> 00:20:21.090
know freedom is the answer/The only question
is…/Who’s next?

00:20:21.090 --> 00:20:25.980
SIERRA: Then, fast forward to 2020, Kendrick
Lamar’s track “Alright” and Childish

00:20:25.980 --> 00:20:31.570
Gambino’s “This is America” became synonymous
with the global 2020 Black Lives Matter marches.

00:20:31.570 --> 00:20:37.690
Kendrick Lamar: This is America/Don't catch
you slippin' now/Don't catch you slippin'

00:20:37.690 --> 00:20:38.690
now

00:20:38.690 --> 00:20:42.559
SIERRA: And in recent months, some of Russia’s
most prominent rappers have been releasing

00:20:42.559 --> 00:20:47.370
tracks that are viscerally critical of the
Russian invasion of Ukraine.

00:20:47.370 --> 00:20:52.789
In one of these songs, star rapper Morgenshtern
sampled a real-life recording of a Ukrainian

00:20:52.789 --> 00:20:57.990
mother telling her son that she was deciding
whether to run or seek shelter from the bombings.

00:20:57.990 --> 00:20:59.240
Palagin’s mother (Russian): Сынулечка,
ну да, у нас тут, прям у нас,

00:20:59.240 --> 00:21:00.280
утром и крышу чуть не снесло
Мы думали куда-то тикать,

00:21:00.280 --> 00:21:01.530
а потом вернулись на место
Ну вот щас сидим, и в погребе

00:21:01.530 --> 00:21:02.530
бомбоубежище себе приготовили
Так что, ну котик, не волнуйся.

00:21:02.530 --> 00:21:03.929
Palagin’s mother (English): My dear son,
well yes, here, right here, in the morning

00:21:03.929 --> 00:21:05.450
the roof was almost blown away, we thought
about running somewhere then we returned to

00:21:05.450 --> 00:21:06.450
our place.

00:21:06.450 --> 00:21:09.429
Well, right now we are sitting in the cellar,
we prepared a bomb shelter for ourselves.

00:21:09.429 --> 00:21:10.750
So, well, oh baby, don't worry.

00:21:10.750 --> 00:21:16.250
SIERRA: How do you think that love for hip-hop
interacts with people's thoughts about the

00:21:16.250 --> 00:21:17.570
United States abroad?

00:21:17.570 --> 00:21:22.419
KATZ: Loving hip hop doesn't automatically
mean you love the US.

00:21:22.419 --> 00:21:24.320
There is no direct connection.

00:21:24.320 --> 00:21:29.080
You can love American culture and hate the
US government.

00:21:29.080 --> 00:21:34.720
You can love Hollywood movies and plot the
destruction of America.

00:21:34.720 --> 00:21:37.779
All of those things are simultaneously possible.

00:21:37.779 --> 00:21:43.510
Where the connection and the real power of
hip hop diplomacy is is in the people-to-people

00:21:43.510 --> 00:21:44.510
connection.

00:21:44.510 --> 00:21:50.210
BLACKMAN: I think it provides a calming effect,
in terms of the way people might feel about

00:21:50.210 --> 00:21:52.210
certain politics.

00:21:52.210 --> 00:21:55.270
And then, there's a softening that occurs
because then you got hip-hop.

00:21:55.270 --> 00:21:57.270
For many it's a religion.

00:21:57.270 --> 00:21:58.770
It is a sport.

00:21:58.770 --> 00:22:00.480
It is a practice.

00:22:00.480 --> 00:22:04.880
It is a part of their essence and who they
are.

00:22:04.880 --> 00:22:08.100
Hip-hop heads don't play about the culture.

00:22:08.100 --> 00:22:14.780
So, I think it does influence them in ways
that allow the government to be strategic,

00:22:14.780 --> 00:22:22.650
to make strategic moves, that allows for younger
people, particularly new generations, to not

00:22:22.650 --> 00:22:29.169
turn against the country, to have more balanced
critiques.

00:22:29.169 --> 00:22:33.960
I think that's one of the things that hip-hop
does, and the hip-hop diplomacy does, is it

00:22:33.960 --> 00:22:39.280
creates room for a balanced critique, particularly
if people have engaged directly with hip-hop

00:22:39.280 --> 00:22:40.280
artists.

00:22:40.280 --> 00:22:45.250
If they've engaged with the actual artists
who they were touching and talking to, and

00:22:45.250 --> 00:22:50.659
looking in the eye, then that does create
a different sort of energy and vibration,

00:22:50.659 --> 00:22:56.130
where there's a separation from the people
and the government, and they start to understand

00:22:56.130 --> 00:23:00.240
that the American people aren't necessarily
in sync with the government at all time.

00:23:00.240 --> 00:23:03.850
And I think that's actually a good thing.

00:23:03.850 --> 00:23:10.679
SIERRA: Global opinion surveys continue to
show highly positive attitudes toward US exports

00:23:10.679 --> 00:23:13.960
like music, movies, and popular culture.

00:23:13.960 --> 00:23:19.650
By contrast, the US is perceived far more
negatively when it comes to things like healthcare

00:23:19.650 --> 00:23:21.870
and the state of its democracy.

00:23:21.870 --> 00:23:27.760
Being a global leader in music can seem like
a small victory - but it’s one that goes

00:23:27.760 --> 00:23:28.900
deep.

00:23:28.900 --> 00:23:33.840
When non-Americans hear hip hop - much of
which is critical of American life and society

00:23:33.840 --> 00:23:39.500
- they are hearing evidence of a government
that allows itself to be criticized.

00:23:39.500 --> 00:23:44.250
As experts on public diplomacy have noted,
that gives the United States an important

00:23:44.250 --> 00:23:49.740
type of credibility, especially in contrast
to authoritarian countries like Russia and

00:23:49.740 --> 00:23:51.280
China.

00:23:51.280 --> 00:23:56.690
Soft power is power through attraction, and
on that front, hip hop has given the U.S.

00:23:56.690 --> 00:24:00.260
a global edge.

00:24:00.260 --> 00:24:07.890
KATZ: What I would love for this episode to
do is to reach people who are skeptical of

00:24:07.890 --> 00:24:16.360
hip hop period but also skeptical of hip hop
as a platform for conducting diplomacy.

00:24:16.360 --> 00:24:23.260
I once spoke with a very liberal Democratic
Senator and told him about Next Level and

00:24:23.260 --> 00:24:24.260
he scoffed.

00:24:24.260 --> 00:24:26.539
He said, "Why would we send hip hop abroad?"

00:24:26.539 --> 00:24:28.000
And what I want to say is because it works.

00:24:28.000 --> 00:24:34.270
If you care about creating goodwill around
the world, if you care about connecting with

00:24:34.270 --> 00:24:40.680
communities that traditionally don't love
the US and traditionally hate US foreign policy,

00:24:40.680 --> 00:24:43.990
then you would be smart to send hip hop artists
abroad.

00:24:43.990 --> 00:24:48.779
SIERRA: Before we end, I would like to share
an experience that I’ve had.

00:24:48.779 --> 00:24:54.540
So, I’ve been lucky enough to travel a lot
and one thing that’s been amazing to me,

00:24:54.540 --> 00:25:00.149
is the amount of people who light up when
I specifically say that I am from Brooklyn,

00:25:00.149 --> 00:25:01.580
New York.

00:25:01.580 --> 00:25:03.789
That has a lot to do with hip hop.

00:25:03.789 --> 00:25:10.770
I have had Biggie, Jay-Z and Lil’ Kim lyrics
sung to me on more than one occasion, and

00:25:10.770 --> 00:25:16.400
even had a few DJ’s respond by throwing
on some classic hip hop hits.

00:25:16.400 --> 00:25:22.919
The deep global love for American culture
is real, and it has been for a long time.

00:25:22.919 --> 00:25:28.190
In previous generations it might have been
the Beach Boys, or Smokey Robinson that provoked

00:25:28.190 --> 00:25:29.920
that special moment.

00:25:29.920 --> 00:25:35.790
Our culture and especially our music connects
us to people around the world who otherwise

00:25:35.790 --> 00:25:38.279
might feel no connection at all.

00:25:38.279 --> 00:25:44.630
And right now, hip hop is one of the key forms
carrying on that tradition.

00:25:44.630 --> 00:25:48.559
And that’s something that deserves our recognition.

00:25:48.559 --> 00:25:55.390
SIERRA: Can you tell us just one story about
your travels as a hip hop diplomat?

00:25:55.390 --> 00:26:02.630
BLACKMAN: In Senegal, people warned me to
be careful because it was, "A Muslim country.

00:26:02.630 --> 00:26:05.679
And the men, be careful as a woman there.

00:26:05.679 --> 00:26:08.641
You're going to be treated by the older...

00:26:08.641 --> 00:26:10.309
Especially the older people."

00:26:10.309 --> 00:26:15.620
I was at the National School of The Arts here
in Dakar, and I was speaking.

00:26:15.620 --> 00:26:20.320
After I finished, there were two older men
in the corner dressed in traditional garb,

00:26:20.320 --> 00:26:22.279
and they called over my translator.

00:26:22.279 --> 00:26:26.990
And they stood there and one of them, he virtually
prayed over me.

00:26:26.990 --> 00:26:28.250
Prayed over me.

00:26:28.250 --> 00:26:32.789
The other one said to the translator, "Tell
her thank you.

00:26:32.789 --> 00:26:36.870
Thank you for coming home as a descendant.

00:26:36.870 --> 00:26:42.930
Thank you for representing and bringing intelligence
to this rap stuff.

00:26:42.930 --> 00:26:49.340
And, you keep doing what you're doing because
what you're doing is important and people

00:26:49.340 --> 00:26:55.250
need to know the positive side of the music
and the culture."

00:26:55.250 --> 00:26:56.270
And I cried.

00:26:56.270 --> 00:26:58.340
It was just so beautiful.

00:26:58.340 --> 00:27:01.320
And it was the opposite of the warnings.

00:27:01.320 --> 00:27:05.730
It was the complete opposite of what I was
warned about.

00:27:05.730 --> 00:27:12.720
SIERRA: My last question for you is, the show
is called Why It Matters, we consistently

00:27:12.720 --> 00:27:18.470
argue that things far away matter to you at
home, things at home matter to people far

00:27:18.470 --> 00:27:20.919
away, whether or not we recognize it.

00:27:20.919 --> 00:27:24.980
So, in that vein, why does hip-hop matter?

00:27:24.980 --> 00:27:31.710
BLACKMAN: Hip-hop matters because it is art,
it is creativity, it is culture, it is connection,

00:27:31.710 --> 00:27:33.000
it is community.

00:27:33.000 --> 00:27:39.460
It is a way for us people around the world,
no matter what language we speak, no matter

00:27:39.460 --> 00:27:44.549
our religion, our race, it is a way for us
to be connected, to be united.

00:27:44.549 --> 00:27:49.860
And, if there's ever been a time in our history
when we need to be more on the same page,

00:27:49.860 --> 00:27:57.500
it is now.

00:27:57.500 --> 00:28:05.809
